    "created_at": "2024-07-01T02:43:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-07-01T02:43:02Z",
    "body": "Although I suspect @annevk is right that we should overhaul downloading more thoroughly, this particular issue is tightly scoped enough that someone could make a good contribution on it in isolation. To do so:\r\n\r\n- Write web platform tests\r\n  - https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/tree/master/html/semantics/links/downloading-resources supposedly contains tests for this, but for some reason it seems to be testing `<a ping>`, not downloading? I'm not sure what's up with that; maybe @rwlbuis can comment.\r\n  - The basic idea is similar though: start a download via `.click()`ing on an `<a download>`, then ping the server to see what headers were sent, with various referrer policies.\r\n  - Alternately, more exhaustive testing might be possible using the framework in https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/master/common/security-features/README.md ; it seems complicated though.\r\n- If there is agreement, or at least 2/3 browser agreement, on using referrer policy, then proceed to update the spec.\r\n  - This involves updating https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#downloading-hyperlinks 's _request_ creation step to add a referrer policy.\r\n  - An example to consider modeling off of is https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#update-the-image-data\r\n- If there is not agreement, then come back to this thread and report the results. We might just decide to not use referrer policy for downloads and document that in the spec. Or we might try to muster browser representative excitement to change browser behavior to respect referrer policy.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
